target: cmd/looper/main.go
verify: go test ./cmd/looper/ -count=1 -run 'TestPushCheck$'
expect: stdout is not the JSON Step 14 reads
why:    a refusal is an ANSWER, and its reason is what the operator acts on — "post the report first", "close the run". Returning the refusal before the JSON is written makes exit 3 a bare "no" with nothing to act on, and indistinguishable in content from the tool failing (1, empty stdout). The original exit-0 contract existed to keep exactly that distinction; exit 3 keeps it only while the JSON is still printed.
--- old
	if _, err := out.Write(b); err != nil {
		return err
	}
--- new
	if !res.Push {
		return errPushRefused
	}
	if _, err := out.Write(b); err != nil {
		return err
	}
